1
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:07,030
Jesus has a man by the hand, and they are walking out of town.

2
00:00:10,580 --> 00:00:19,128
Before that, though, stand in the town a while, because the man we are following
knew it without seeing any of it.

3
00:00:19,178 --> 00:00:28,387
Bethsaida is a fishing town, and a fishing town tells you what it is before you look
at it. Gulls, all day.

4
00:00:28,437 --> 00:00:32,303
Water slapping the hulls of the boats drawn up on the shingle.

5
00:00:32,353 --> 00:00:42,188
Men calling to each other across the beach about the night's catch, in that flat,
tired half-shout of people who have been working since before dawn.

6
00:00:42,238 --> 00:00:49,650
The long dry rasp of a net dragged over stone. Wood knocking against wood somewhere
behind you.

7
00:00:51,200 --> 00:01:02,148
And the smell of the place, which is two smells at once. Fish in the water, and fish
drying on the racks in the sun. They are not the same smell.

8
00:01:02,198 --> 00:01:06,990
Anyone who lived there could tell you which way the wind had turned without lifting
their head.

9
00:01:08,540 --> 00:01:18,628
A blind man in a town like that is not lost, and he is not helpless. He knows it the
way you know your own house in the dark.

10
00:01:18,678 --> 00:01:28,422
The street is packed earth, beaten hard by every foot that has gone down it since
morning. He knows where it narrows and where it opens out again.

11
00:01:28,472 --> 00:01:39,443
He knows which wall throws his footsteps back at him and which doorway swallows
them. He knows the turns by what he can smell at each one.

12
00:01:39,493 --> 00:01:41,678
He is not feeling his way along.

13
00:01:41,728 --> 00:01:50,810
He is walking a road he has walked more times than he could count, in a town that
has been telling him where he is for as long as he has needed it to.

14
00:01:52,860 --> 00:01:58,235
Hold on to that, because he is about to let go of it.

15
00:01:58,285 --> 00:02:08,052
He is about to put his hand into the hand of a man he has never touched, and be
walked out past the last house, onto ground he has never learned,

16
00:02:08,102 --> 00:02:11,370
where not one of those things helps him any more.

17
00:02:13,420 --> 00:02:24,970
Today there is something else in it. A crowd, somewhere down the hill, and a crowd
moves all one way, and the noise of this one is going toward the water.

18
00:02:26,520 --> 00:02:37,910
In a minute Jesus is going to do something He does nowhere else in the Gospels. He
is going to heal a man, and then ask him whether it worked.

19
00:02:42,460 --> 00:02:52,144
This is Not By Sight, a series on blindness in the Bible, made by a blind Christian
for blind believers, their families, and the church.

20
00:02:52,194 --> 00:03:01,352
The Scripture you will hear is the Berean Standard Bible, read word for word. The
thoughts marked as Brian's are Brian Clark's own.

21
00:03:01,402 --> 00:03:04,410
And when we imagine a scene, we will tell you so.

22
00:03:06,060 --> 00:03:16,994
First, where we are, and it matters more than usual for this one. Jesus has just fed
four thousand people on the far side of the Sea of Galilee.

23
00:03:17,044 --> 00:03:27,175
He gets into a boat with His disciples, and on the water He has an argument with
them. Mark tells it just before the healing, and the two belong together.

24
00:03:27,225 --> 00:03:33,290
Listen for the word eyes. Mark chapter 8, verses 14 to 21.

25
00:03:34,140 --> 00:03:43,267
[Reader] Now the disciples had forgotten to take bread, except for one loaf they had
with them in the boat. “Watch out!” He cautioned them.

26
00:03:43,317 --> 00:03:52,728
[Reader] “Beware of the leaven of the Pharisees and of Herod.” So they began to
discuss with one another the fact that they had no bread.

27
00:03:52,778 --> 00:04:03,672
[Reader] Aware of their conversation, Jesus asked them, “Why are you debating about
having no bread? Do you still not see or understand? Do you have such hard hearts?

28
00:04:03,722 --> 00:04:11,063
[Reader] ‘Having eyes, do you not see? And having ears, do you not hear?’ And do you
not remember?

29
00:04:11,113 --> 00:04:20,100
[Reader] When I broke the five loaves for the five thousand, how many basketfuls of
broken pieces did you collect?” “Twelve,” they answered.

30
00:04:20,150 --> 00:04:29,227
[Reader] “And when I broke the seven loaves for the four thousand, how many
basketfuls of broken pieces did you collect?” “Seven,” they said.

31
00:04:29,277 --> 00:04:32,890
[Reader] Then He asked them, “Do you still not understand?”

32
00:04:34,940 --> 00:04:43,842
Having eyes, do you not see? He says it to His disciples, men whose eyes worked
perfectly well.

33
00:04:43,892 --> 00:04:53,130
Then the boat lands at Bethsaida, and the first thing that happens is this. Mark
chapter 8, verses 22 to 26.

34
00:04:54,180 --> 00:05:06,746
[Reader] When they arrived at Bethsaida, some people brought a blind man and begged
Jesus to touch him. So He took the blind man by the hand and led him out of the
village.

35
00:05:06,796 --> 00:05:14,372
[Reader] Then He spit on the man’s eyes and placed His hands on him. “Can you see
anything?” He asked.

36
00:05:14,422 --> 00:05:21,311
[Reader] The man looked up and said, “I can see the people, but they look like trees
walking around.”

37
00:05:21,361 --> 00:05:31,271
[Reader] Once again Jesus placed His hands on the man’s eyes, and when he opened
them his sight was restored, and he could see everything clearly.

38
00:05:31,321 --> 00:05:35,090
[Reader] Jesus sent him home and said, “Do not go back into the village.”

39
00:05:36,640 --> 00:05:41,159
Mark puts the two stories side by side on purpose.

40
00:05:41,209 --> 00:05:53,070
Sighted men who cannot see what is in front of them, and a blind man who, halfway
through, sees a little and says so honestly. We will come back to that.

41
00:05:58,120 --> 00:06:03,559
The place. Bethsaida means house of fish.

42
00:06:03,609 --> 00:06:13,610
It was a fishing town on the north shore of the Sea of Galilee, near where the
Jordan runs in, and it was the home town of three of the twelve: Andrew,

43
00:06:13,660 --> 00:06:14,742
Peter and Philip.

44
00:06:14,792 --> 00:06:25,864
Philip the tetrarch, Herod's son, raised it to the rank of a city and renamed it
Julias after a woman of the emperor's family, Josephus says his daughter,

45
00:06:25,914 --> 00:06:30,920
so in Jesus's day it was half fishing village and half new Roman town.

46
00:06:30,970 --> 00:06:33,879
Where exactly it stood is still argued.

47
00:06:33,929 --> 00:06:43,079
Archaeologists have dug two candidate ruins on that shore, and at both they have
found fishing weights and the needles used for mending nets.

48
00:06:43,129 --> 00:06:56,097
Whichever ruin is right, the sounds would have been the same: water, boats being
hauled, nets, gulls, and the particular silence of a lake in the early morning.

49
00:06:56,147 --> 00:07:05,475
Edersheim calls this the only gradual cure in the Gospels, and notes that saliva was
a well known Jewish remedy for the eyes.

50
00:07:05,525 --> 00:07:13,064
From the man's words about trees he infers that the blindness was not from birth but
came from disease.

51
00:07:13,114 --> 00:07:24,572
That inference is his, not the text's, though a man who knows what a tree looks like
has seen one. Why did Jesus lead him out of the village? The text does not say.

52
00:07:24,622 --> 00:07:34,880
Matthew Henry points out that Jesus could have healed him privately in a house, so
the walk out of town must mean something, and suggests two things:

53
00:07:34,930 --> 00:07:44,980
that Jesus did the leading Himself to teach us to be as Job was, eyes to the blind,
and that Bethsaida had forfeited the sight of another miracle.

54
00:07:45,030 --> 00:07:55,437
Edersheim adds that the many steps, the leading, the saliva, the hands laid on
twice, were there to rule out any idea of a magic cure;

55
00:07:55,487 --> 00:07:58,714
everything centred on the person doing it.

56
00:07:58,764 --> 00:08:08,441
And both of them connect the walk with the woes Jesus pronounced on Bethsaida, which
Matthew records. One thing to have straight before we read it.

57
00:08:08,491 --> 00:08:17,898
These woes are not about the man we are following. Matthew says what they are about:
the towns where most of Jesus's miracles had already been done.

58
00:08:17,948 --> 00:08:23,613
Bethsaida had seen many of them, and almost none of them were written down anywhere.

59
00:08:23,663 --> 00:08:35,350
Whatever the healing on the road was for, it was not the evidence against the town.
That evidence was already in. Matthew chapter 11, verses 20 to 24.

60
00:08:36,400 --> 00:08:50,352
[Reader] Then Jesus began to denounce the cities in which most of His miracles had
been performed, because they did not repent. “Woe to you, Chorazin! Woe to you,
Bethsaida!

61
00:08:50,402 --> 00:09:00,050
[Reader] For if the miracles that were performed in you had been performed in Tyre
and Sidon, they would have repented long ago in sackcloth and ashes.

62
00:09:00,100 --> 00:09:10,315
[Reader] But I tell you, it will be more bearable for Tyre and Sidon on the day of
judgment than for you. And you, Capernaum, will you be lifted up to heaven?

63
00:09:10,365 --> 00:09:21,225
[Reader] No, you will be brought down to Hades! For if the miracles that were
performed in you had been performed in Sodom, it would have remained to this day.

64
00:09:21,275 --> 00:09:26,430
[Reader] But I tell you that it will be more bearable for Sodom on the day of
judgment than for you.”

65
00:09:27,980 --> 00:09:40,273
A town that had seen so much and believed so little. Jesus takes the man away from
it, heals him outside it, and tells him not to go back into it.

66
00:09:40,323 --> 00:09:44,810
Whatever else that means, it means the healing was not a show for Bethsaida.

67
00:09:46,860 --> 00:09:58,582
Scripture tells us he was brought, that Jesus led him by the hand, and what he said
halfway through. It does not tell us who brought him or what the walk was like.

68
00:09:58,632 --> 00:10:05,930
We imagine a man who had once seen and had learned to live without it. This is a
scene, not Scripture.

69
00:10:09,980 --> 00:10:16,010
[Friend] He is here. The boat is in. Come on, up, take my arm, they are all going
down to the shore.

70
00:10:17,560 --> 00:10:23,270
[Man] Slow down. I know the way to the shore. Left at the net racks, the ground
drops after the last house.

71
00:10:25,320 --> 00:10:30,710
[Friend] Teacher! This man, our friend. Touch him. Please. Just touch him.

72
00:10:33,896 --> 00:10:48,742
[Man] A hand takes mine. Not my friend's hand. Warmer, and not in a hurry. We walk.
The crowd noise falls behind us like a door closing. Street, then dirt, then grass.

73
00:10:48,792 --> 00:11:04,029
[Man] The lake is on my right. I can smell it and hear it. He stops. He touches my
eyes, and his hands stay there. Then he asks me, do you see anything. I see. I see
people.

74
00:11:04,079 --> 00:11:12,670
[Man] But they are like trees walking. Tall, moving, no faces. He does not say
anything. He puts his hands on my eyes again.

75
00:11:14,720 --> 00:11:16,750
[Man] Oh. Oh, there is the lake.

76
00:11:18,800 --> 00:11:28,990
Mark says he looked intently and his sight was restored and he saw everything
clearly. Three verbs for one moment of seeing.

77
00:11:32,540 --> 00:11:44,368
A word about the voice you are about to hear. Brian decided he was too shy to record
his own thoughts. We suspect he was being a scaredy cat.

78
00:11:44,418 --> 00:11:54,970
So, against our better judgment, we let him pick his own voice. Whenever you hear
it, from here to the end, these are his words.

79
00:11:56,520 --> 00:12:06,107
[Brian] People like trees walking. Of every line about blindness in the Bible, this
is the one my low-vision friends can relate to most. They know exactly what he
means.

80
00:12:06,157 --> 00:12:14,949
[Brian] Shapes that move, that are the right height to be people, but with no faces
and no edges. You can see something, and you cannot trust it.

81
00:12:14,999 --> 00:12:25,211
[Brian] The Bible has one word for blindness, and most of the time it means the deep
end of the pool, where I swim. This verse is the one time it describes the middle.

82
00:12:25,261 --> 00:12:34,558
[Brian] Jesus asked him how it was going. Think about that. He had put His hands on
the man's eyes, and instead of announcing a result, He asked. And the man told the
truth.

83
00:12:34,608 --> 00:12:44,976
[Brian] Not yet. It is better, but not yet. Half the trouble blind people get into
with well-meaning helpers is that we are too polite to say not yet.

84
00:12:45,026 --> 00:12:49,910
[Brian] The man at Bethsaida said it to Jesus, and Jesus did not mind. He put His
hands on him again.

85
00:12:52,960 --> 00:13:02,310
Back to the boat for a moment, because Mark wants us there. Jesus asked His
disciples, having eyes, do you not see?

86
00:13:02,360 --> 00:13:14,790
Then He lands and meets a man who, after the first touch, sees a little and says so.
The disciples had seen two miracles of bread and still did not understand.

87
00:13:14,840 --> 00:13:22,788
The blind man had seen shapes like trees and understood exactly how much he could
and could not see.

88
00:13:22,838 --> 00:13:33,223
Mark is making a point about which of them was really blind, and it is not the
beggar. The commentators have always read it that way.

89
00:13:33,273 --> 00:13:41,066
Henry calls the disciples' blindness a matter of dullness, not eyes. And the chapter
does not end here.

90
00:13:41,116 --> 00:13:52,763
Right after this, Jesus asks the disciples who people say He is, and Peter answers,
You are the Christ. Peter sees clearly.

91
00:13:52,813 --> 00:14:02,670
Then Jesus tells them He must suffer and die, and Peter rebukes Him. Trees walking.
Peter, too, saw in two stages.

92
00:14:04,220 --> 00:14:08,310
[Brian] Jesus led him by the hand. Not a disciple, not the friends who brought him.

93
00:14:08,360 --> 00:14:18,945
[Brian] The same Jesus who was about to put mud on another man's eyes and send him
walking across Jerusalem alone took this man by the hand and walked him out of the
village Himself.

94
00:14:18,995 --> 00:14:20,125
[Brian] Both are right.

95
00:14:20,175 --> 00:14:29,722
[Brian] Some days you need to be sent, and some days you need a hand or an arm, and
the real trick is knowing which days you need a hand and which days you don't.

96
00:14:29,772 --> 00:14:36,124
[Brian] He knew what trees looked like. That means he had seen before. Like
Bartimaeus, like me, he lost it.

97
00:14:36,174 --> 00:14:43,821
[Brian] I have noticed that the Gospels never explain how anyone lost their sight,
and I have come to be grateful for that. Nobody asked him what happened.

98
00:14:43,871 --> 00:14:45,690
[Brian] Jesus just asked whether he could see.

99
00:14:50,240 --> 00:14:55,265
[Brian] Let me tell you what it is actually like to take a stranger's arm. You do
not know how they walk.

100
00:14:55,315 --> 00:14:59,021
[Brian] You do not know whether they will tell you about the kerb or let you find
it.

101
00:14:59,071 --> 00:15:08,924
[Brian] Most people mean well and have no idea how to do it, and you spend the whole
walk managing them, a half step behind, reading the arm for what the mouth forgot to
say.

102
00:15:08,974 --> 00:15:17,275
[Brian] Every blind person knows that walk. It is not restful. You are trusting
someone who has not earned it yet, because the alternative is not moving.

103
00:15:17,325 --> 00:15:22,927
[Brian] Now think about this man. He had a town he could cross without help, and he
let go of it.

104
00:15:22,977 --> 00:15:30,133
[Brian] He let a man he had never touched take his hand and walk him out past the
last house, onto ground he had never learned.

105
00:15:30,183 --> 00:15:35,844
[Brian] And I do not think he spent that walk managing anybody. I think he knew, the
way you know, that this hand was not going to let him trip.

106
00:15:35,894 --> 00:15:42,350
[Brian] That is the part that undoes me. Not the eyes. The hand.

107
00:15:44,900 --> 00:15:50,490
[Brian] I am saying this next part while being in the middle of something, not from
having reached the far shore.

108
00:15:50,540 --> 00:16:00,769
[Brian] Something we depend on has broken this week, we cannot fix it, and we do not
yet know how it gets replaced. So take what follows as a report, not as advice.

109
00:16:00,819 --> 00:16:11,786
[Brian] My family is three disabled people, and since going blind 12 years ago, I
have not watched a need go unmet. Not once. And it has even been a time of great
plenty.

110
00:16:11,836 --> 00:16:17,222
[Brian] It was only after going blind that I was given my own home and many of the
comforts we enjoy.

111
00:16:17,272 --> 00:16:23,593
[Brian] Looking back, I can see God's providence as clearly as the man saw the lake
at the end of this story.

112
00:16:23,643 --> 00:16:28,684
[Brian] Looking forward, from where I am standing this week, I see only trees.

113
00:16:28,734 --> 00:16:32,930
[Brian] That is the honest position, and I have come to think it is the one this
story is actually about.

114
00:16:35,480 --> 00:16:42,630
[Brian] I may not always be able to see the path clearly, but I always know I can
put my hand in His and trust Him to lead the way.

115
00:16:44,680 --> 00:16:53,309
A last word for anyone listening who lives in the middle of the pool, with some
sight and not enough.

116
00:16:53,359 --> 00:17:03,614
The Bible notices you once, here, in one sentence, and the sentence is true. Men
like trees walking.

117
00:17:03,664 --> 00:17:09,961
It does not pretend you see nothing, and it does not pretend you see well.

118
00:17:10,011 --> 00:17:18,077
And in the story, the man who said his sight was not yet right was not told to be
satisfied with it.

119
00:17:18,127 --> 00:17:27,110
Jesus laid His hands on him again, the same hands that would soon be nailed to a
cross, and made his sight whole.

120
00:17:29,160 --> 00:17:30,790
Jesus makes it right.

121
00:17:34,024 --> 00:17:43,394
This has been Not By Sight. The full story, with every source named, is at
blindnessinthebible.com.

122
00:17:43,444 --> 00:17:54,502
Scripture quotations are from the Holy Bible, Berean Standard Bible, which is in the
public domain. The scenes you heard marked as imagined are our own.

123
00:17:54,552 --> 00:18:00,990
Everything else is from the text and the old books. For we walk by faith, not by
sight.

